"""English companion to lessons.json. Keep lesson IDs and section order aligned."""
import json
from pathlib import Path

def section(title, body, example, takeaway):
    return dict(title=title, body=body, example=example, takeaway=takeaway)

translations = [
('Think Agile before starting Scrum',
 'Understand why short cycles help, and shift your attention from completing a plan to delivering something valuable.', [
section('Agile: responding to uncertainty',
 'Software teams rarely know everything on day one. User needs and possible solutions can change. Agile uses short cycles to deliver working software, collect feedback, and adjust what happens next.\nAgile still involves planning. The difference is that plans change as the team learns. Attending every meeting is not enough if nothing usable is delivered and no improvements follow.',
 'A room-booking team first lets students select a room and reserve a time. Feedback reveals that overlapping bookings are the real problem, so the team prioritizes preventing conflicts before building profile pages.',
 'Measure progress through working software and the value it creates for users.'),
section('How to read the four Agile values',
 'Agile values individuals and interactions over processes and tools; working software over comprehensive documentation; customer collaboration over contract negotiation; and responding to change over following a plan.\nThe items on the right still have value. Teams still document, use tools, and plan. These activities should support delivery rather than become obstacles to it.',
 'A team has a beautiful task board but never discusses requirements. Members understand the same feature differently. Another tool will not solve this: the team first needs a shared understanding of the goal and acceptance conditions.',
 'Tools support collaboration; they cannot replace shared understanding.'),
section('Principles that change everyday work',
 'Deliver valuable software frequently, collaborate regularly with stakeholders, and welcome changes in requirements. These practices expose misunderstandings before the team invests too much in the wrong direction.\nTechnical excellence, simplicity, a sustainable pace, and regular reflection also matter. If every delivery leaves code harder to change, the team gradually loses its ability to adapt.',
 'The team notices that manual testing consumes too much time. It adds automated tests for the main booking flow, making changes in the next cycle safer and more reliable.',
 'Agile needs both collaboration and engineering capability.'),
section('Timeboxing: fix the time, choose the scope',
 'A timebox is an agreed limit. When time runs out, the activity ends instead of expanding indefinitely to finish every item. The team must prioritize and choose a scope that fits its capacity.\nThe classroom approach fixes time, cost, and agreed quality, then uses feature priorities to manage risk. Quietly reducing quality is not an acceptable response to running out of time.',
 'With three people and two weeks, finish the core booking flow to the agreed quality first. Add theme customization only if time remains. Defer the theme instead of removing conflict checks.',
 'Negotiate scope openly rather than silently lowering quality.'),
section('The three pillars of empiricism',
 'Scrum builds knowledge from experience. Transparency means making information visible and understood consistently. Inspection means examining what is happening against a goal. Adaptation means adjusting when something deviates from expectations.\nThe pillars work together. Inaccurate information produces misleading inspection; inspecting without adapting wastes the learning opportunity.',
 'A board says a feature is finished even though it has not been tested. Agreeing on the meaning of Done makes the information trustworthy, so the team can assess real progress.',
 'Transparency → inspection → adaptation is a connected learning cycle.')]),
('See how Scrum works as a whole',
 'Connect the people, work, and events to understand how each part supports valuable delivery.', [
section('Scrum is a framework',
 'Scrum provides a lightweight structure for tackling complex problems. It does not prescribe every coding step. Teams still choose techniques and practices that fit their situation.\nIts central cycle is the Sprint. Each Sprint has a goal and creates a usable Increment, giving the team evidence for learning and future decisions.',
 'Scrum does not choose React or a database design for you. The team chooses its technology, while making sure the integrated result meets its agreed quality standard.',
 'Understand the purpose of the framework before choosing tools.'),
section('Three accountabilities in the Scrum Team',
 'The Product Owner is accountable for maximizing product value and effective Product Backlog management. Developers create a usable Increment each Sprint. The Scrum Master helps people understand Scrum and use it effectively.\nThese accountabilities work together. The Product Owner does not dictate coding methods, and the Scrum Master is not a boss assigning tasks to individuals. Developers manage their plan together.',
 'The Product Owner explains why preventing double bookings matters. Developers explore technical options. The Scrum Master helps the team collaborate and address impediments.',
 'Keep accountabilities clear while working as one team.'),
section('Artifacts make work visible',
 'The Product Backlog is an ordered list of work to improve the product, with a Product Goal as its longer-term objective. The Sprint Backlog contains the Sprint Goal, selected items, and the delivery plan. An Increment is a usable result that meets the Definition of Done.\nDo not confuse intended work with completed work. Code that has been written but has not met the agreed quality standard is not a Done Increment.',
 '“Let students book rooms independently” could be the Product Goal. “Let students see available times and confirm a reservation” could be the first Sprint Goal.',
 'The Product Goal, Sprint Goal, and Definition of Done make inspection meaningful.'),
section('How events connect within a Sprint',
 'Sprint Planning establishes the goal and plan. During the Sprint, Developers build the product and use the Daily Scrum to inspect progress. Near the end, the Sprint Review examines the outcome and product direction with stakeholders; the Sprint Retrospective improves how the team works.\nThe Sprint contains these events. Development happens throughout the cycle, rather than waiting until every meeting is over.',
 'The team plans on Monday, develops and adjusts daily, then invites stakeholders to try the booking system. It also discusses how to reduce delays in code review next Sprint.',
 'Each event inspects something different and supports a different kind of adaptation.'),
section('Review versus Retrospective',
 'The Sprint Review examines product outcomes, progress toward goals, and what to do next in collaboration with stakeholders. It is a working session, rather than simply a presentation for a grade.\nThe Sprint Retrospective examines quality and effectiveness: people, interactions, practices, tools, and the Definition of Done. The team identifies improvements it can act on.',
 'Review: users need building names in the room list → adjust the backlog. Retrospective: integration was left until the final day → agree to integrate work daily.',
 'Review adapts product direction; Retrospective improves the way the team works.')]),
('Turn needs into a Product Backlog',
 'Move from broad ideas to pieces of work that the team can understand, estimate, and order by value.', [
section('The Product Backlog evolves',
 'The Product Backlog is a source of work for improving the product. It evolves as the team learns. Each Product Backlog Item, or PBI, may represent a feature, a fix, or a necessary improvement.\nItems near the top should be clear and small enough for near-term decisions. Distant work can remain broad. The Product Owner is accountable for backlog management and collaborates with Developers and stakeholders.',
 '“Room management system” is too broad to develop as one item. As it approaches implementation, the team splits it into viewing availability, making reservations, and cancelling reservations.',
 'Add detail when it becomes useful for a decision.'),
section('User stories start with people and value',
 'A useful thinking pattern is: “As a [user], I want [capability], so that [benefit].” The benefit explains why the capability matters, instead of merely naming a screen or database table.\nA story starts a conversation; it does not replace all requirements. Discuss scenarios, constraints, and acceptance conditions together. Not every PBI has to be a user story.',
 'As a student, I want to see available room times so that I can choose a booking without repeatedly submitting requests that fail.',
 'Explain who benefits from a feature and how.'),
section('Order work by value and necessity',
 'MoSCoW distinguishes Must have, Should have, Could have, and Won’t have this time. Categories help communicate necessity, but the Product Owner must also order items within each category.\nIf everything is a Must have, the team has no flexibility when problems arise. Identify the smallest useful set that can be delivered, while considering risks and dependencies.',
 'Must: make bookings without time conflicts. Should: cancel bookings. Could: customize theme colors. When time is short, defer the theme; a system that creates overlapping reservations still misses the goal.',
 'Prioritization decides both what to do and what to do first.'),
section('Refinement prepares work for selection',
 'Product Backlog refinement continuously breaks work down, builds understanding, and adds useful detail, such as description, order, and size. It does not have to wait until Sprint Planning.\nThe Product Owner explains needs and value; Developers contribute technical understanding and sizing. If the team uses a Definition of Ready, treat it as a local agreement about readiness, rather than a mandatory Scrum artifact.',
 '“Make a booking” leaves questions unanswered: how far ahead can students book, who may cancel, and how are simultaneous requests handled? The team discusses these before selecting the item.',
 'Ready means enough understanding to begin, not complete certainty.'),
section('Estimate to support planning',
 'Sizing helps compare complexity, effort, and uncertainty. It is not a guarantee of an exact completion time. Developers doing the work should participate in sizing it.\nLarge or unclear items may need splitting or investigation. Past delivery data can inform forecasts, but current capacity and context still matter. Avoid using estimates to rank different teams.',
 'Showing a list of rooms is familiar work. Preventing concurrent requests from double-booking a room introduces more uncertainty, even if the screen has only one button.',
 'Screen size is not work size, and an estimate is not a guaranteed deadline.')]),
('Make the Sprint deliver',
 'Set a goal, select realistic work, and turn progress into something usable within the cycle.', [
section('Sprint Planning answers Why / What / How',
 'Why: what value should this Sprint create, and what is the Sprint Goal? What: which PBIs can fit the team’s capacity? How: how will Developers create the Increment, including any useful task breakdown?\nThe goal guides decisions during the Sprint. If the original plan no longer works, Developers discuss scope with the Product Owner while protecting the goal, rather than accepting every new request immediately.',
 'Goal: students can reserve rooms without conflicts. The team selects availability and confirmation items, then breaks them into UI, API, and concurrency-testing tasks.',
 'The Sprint Goal explains why these items belong together.'),
section('Acceptance Criteria and the Definition of Done',
 'Acceptance Criteria describe item-specific behavior. The Definition of Done describes the shared quality standard that the Increment must meet.\nPassing a few functional conditions does not make an item Done if integration, testing, or another agreed standard is missing. Work that fails the Definition of Done is not part of the Increment and returns to the Product Backlog for future consideration.',
 'Acceptance Criterion: if a time slot is already booked, another booking must not be created. DoD: code reviewed, agreed tests passed, and the feature integrated with the existing system.',
 'Criteria describe specific behavior; Done establishes shared quality.'),
section('Flow management: finish before starting more',
 'When everyone starts more work at once, many items remain in progress while little becomes usable. Look at the flow of each PBI from start to Done, reducing waiting and handoffs.\nSwarming means helping complete an item that has already started, by taking remaining tasks or removing obstacles. It does not require everyone to edit the same line. Pair programming can also support collaboration and shared understanding.',
 'After finishing the UI, a developer helps with tests and API integration instead of immediately opening another PBI. This helps the first booking feature reach Done sooner.',
 'Value comes from finished work, not the amount of work started.'),
section('Use the Daily Scrum to adapt the daily plan',
 'The Daily Scrum is a short event for Developers to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal and adapt the Sprint Backlog into an actionable plan. The team chooses a useful format.\nIt is not a status report to a manager. Detailed problem-solving can continue afterward with the relevant people. Impediments should be raised when discovered, rather than saved for tomorrow’s meeting.',
 '“The booking API has a transaction issue that threatens the goal. Two of us will investigate today while another prepares tests” is more useful than listing yesterday’s coding hours.',
 'Leave the Daily Scrum knowing how to move forward together.'),
section('Read the board to spot problems',
 'A To do / In progress / Done board makes status visible, but only if people update it and share the same definitions. Track PBIs as well as tasks: many completed tasks do not necessarily mean usable delivery.\nWhen work stalls, ask what it is waiting for, who can help, and how it affects the Sprint Goal. Adapt the plan instead of waiting until the Review to reveal that the work is not ready.',
 'Four of five tasks are complete, but the final task tests booking conflicts. Until that task and the agreed quality checks pass, the PBI should not move to Done.',
 'Status should reflect reality well enough to support decisions.')]),
('Build a team that trusts and improves',
 'Scrum values appear in everyday behavior, from helping teammates to speaking openly about problems.', [
section('The five Scrum values in practice',
 'Commitment: support goals and the team. Focus: concentrate on important Sprint work. Openness: share work and challenges. Respect: value other people’s capabilities and perspectives. Courage: do the right thing and address difficult problems.\nThese values build trust and make transparency, inspection, and adaptation possible. They should shape behavior, rather than remain words on a wall.',
 'A developer realizes an estimate was too optimistic and tells the team immediately. Others listen without blame, help adapt the plan, and decide what to discuss with the Product Owner.',
 'Speaking openly about problems takes courage, respect, and trust.'),
section('Working group versus working team',
 'A working group often divides tasks and combines the results. A working team also shares a goal, complementary skills, and mutual accountability for the overall outcome, while retaining individual responsibility.\n“My part is finished” is not the end if the team’s Increment still cannot be used. Helping with a bottleneck is part of teamwork.',
 'The frontend is ready, but the API is blocked. A team focused on its shared goal helps test, investigate, and reduce the remaining work so users can actually book rooms.',
 'Take responsibility for your own contribution and the team’s outcome.'),
section('Self-managing and cross-functional',
 'Self-managing means the team decides internally who does what, when, and how within its shared goals. Cross-functional means the team collectively has the skills needed to create value without handing every step to an outside specialist.\nEveryone does not need identical expertise. People should learn, collaborate, and reduce bottlenecks caused by depending on one person. Pairing and knowledge-sharing help build that capability.',
 'Only one person knows how to deploy. The team pairs on deployments and writes a short guide so others can step in when that person is unavailable.',
 'Team autonomy depends on shared capabilities and shared decisions.'),
section('Technical debt and refactoring',
 'Technical debt is the future burden created by technical choices that make changes harder or more expensive. A team may deliberately accept debt for a short-term benefit, but should make the cost visible and plan how to manage it.\nRefactoring improves internal structure without changing observable behavior, for example by removing duplication, extracting functions, or improving names. Technical debt does not justify calling work Done when it fails the Definition of Done.',
 'Booking-conflict checks are duplicated in three places. Changing a rule requires editing all three. The team extracts a shared function and uses tests to confirm that behavior remains unchanged.',
 'Internal quality protects the ability to change the product over time.'),
section('The Scrum Master helps the team think and improve',
 'The Scrum Master coaches self-management and cross-functionality, helps Scrum events become useful, and helps address impediments to progress.\nCoaching uses questions to help the team explore problems and options rather than dictating every answer. Support for the Product Owner and organization should respect their accountabilities, without taking over product ordering or technical decisions.',
 'Instead of ordering overtime, the Scrum Master asks where work is waiting and whether limiting work in progress or swarming could help. The team then experiments with its chosen approach.',
 'Help the team become able to decide and improve for itself.')])]

base = json.loads(Path('lessons.json').read_text())
for lesson, (title, intro, sections) in zip(base, translations, strict=True):
    lesson.update(title=title, intro=intro, sections=sections)
Path('lessons.en.json').write_text(json.dumps(base, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
print('English content: 5 lessons, 25 sections')
